podcast. So let's get started, shall we?

99
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:50,840
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

100
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:03,960
You know I have seen that worried look. so many times

101
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:08,720
It happens just after parents have brought their scout down to

102
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:13,640
camp and they have gone in and they see the tent that he's going to be

103
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:17,280
staying in and they kind of look around a little bit and they look at me

104
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:19,760
and the other adults are going to be staying there during the week

105
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:25,760
and they kind of reluctantly start dragging their feet up the trail

106
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:32,640
and they look a little uncertain that they're ready to do this themselves.

107
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:36,280
Most of the time the scouts. they're not even thinking right

108
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:40,840
Because they're at camp, they're happy, they're there with their buddies

109
00:07:40,840 --> 00:07:42,280
and they're looking forward to a great week.

110
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:47,040
But parents sometimes, hey, this can catch them by surprise

111
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:51,120
because they concentrate a lot on preparing their scout to go to camp

112
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:56,000
but they may be unaware of the fact that, hey, this is going to hit them

113
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:02,440
a little bit. They are leaving their child at a camp in the care of people

114
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:06,240
who they may know, and really well, and maybe not know really well,

115
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:11,240
and they may feel a little uncomfortable turning over full time

116
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:16,080
care of their child to somebody who they have just gotten to know

117
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:21,880
and they're going to worry about what kind of food the scout will be eating

118
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:25,440
and who will be looking after daily concerns like clean clothes

119
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:29,360
and taking showers and making sure they eat and making sure they don't get hurt.

120
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:33,440
And they kind of wonder if the older scouts are going to be nice

121
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:34,760
or if they're going to tease them.

122
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:37,400
And hey, you know, if you've had this experience,

123
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:39,240
I really don't have to explain it to you.

124
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:42,000
I mean, what happens if you take your child to camp

125
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,840
and they just don't like camp?

126
00:08:43,840 --> 00:08:47,480
Can they call home or can you check in on them?

127
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:50,680
Oh, it can be really, really anxious,

128
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:54,520
And I have seen this anxiety in parents of scouts headed to camp

129
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:56,960
for the first time for many, many years.

130
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:01,800
Many of those parents find the experience more difficult

131
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:03,000
than their children do,

132
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,680
And if they wait for the first contact from camp, the first letter home,

133
00:09:08,120 --> 00:09:11,280
they don't find a whole lot of solace in that either,

134
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:14,240
because it may sound pretty miserable

135
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:16,840
even though a scout's having the time of his life.

136
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:22,680
So the question then becomes: how can we scouts help parents prepare

137
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:26,320
for how they're going to feel once they've dropped their scouts

138
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:30,280
off at camp and returned home for a very, very quiet

139
00:09:30,520 --> 00:09:32,200
and sometimes anxious week?

140
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:35,040
Being separated from your child for this length of time

141
00:09:35,040 --> 00:09:39,680
is a new challenge for every parent, even though many others

142
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:42,800
have gone through it before and try to be as supportive as possible.

143
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:46,520
You know it really comes down to the way that you end up feeling

144
00:09:46,640 --> 00:09:50,720
and acknowledging that this causes most parents at least

145
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:54,560
a little bit of anxiety and distress, that it's a normal part

146
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:57,600
of your child growing up, that it's temporary

147
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:00,680
and you're going to feel better about it is going to help.

148
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,840
As somebody who's gone to summer camp for decades,

149
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:07,640
I understand that most scouts, especially younger ones

150
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:10,680
will experience some level of homesickness

151
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:12,280
and they're going to experience some ups and downs

152
00:10:12,280 --> 00:10:14,560
because camp is challenging and it ought to be.

153
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:15,840
It's a great challenge for them.

154
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:19,120
But what you need to understand as a parent, is that

155
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:22,840
his fellow scouts and the scouters that are with him

156
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:26,720
and the camp staff are all working hard to make this

157
00:10:26,720 --> 00:10:28,960
challenge a positive experience,

158
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:34,200
And scouts get a lot out of it year after year after year.

159
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:37,560
While you're concentrating on getting your scout ready

160
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:40,840
to go to camp, prepare yourself too.

161
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:42,920
Here's some ways that you can do just that.

162
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:46,960
The first one sounds pretty easy to understand,

163
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:49,720
but it bears a little explanation, and that is

164
00:10:49,720 --> 00:10:51,800
first of all, as a parent. think positively.

165
00:10:52,240 --> 00:10:56,120
The overwhelming percentage of scouts have a typically

166
00:10:56,120 --> 00:10:57,920
great time at summer camp.

167
00:10:57,920 --> 00:11:01,360
They absolutely thrive on it and they come home happy.

168
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:04,680
Your positive attitude about that is important,

169
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:06,680
because if you're going to be anxious about it

170
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:07,720
and you're going to worry about it,

171
00:11:07,720 --> 00:11:09,680
you're going to communicate that to them

172
00:11:09,720 --> 00:11:12,120
during the preparations. whether you like it or not,

173
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:13,880
they're going to kind of get that message.

174
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:16,320
So, instead of discussing your worries

175
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:18,120
or how much you're going to miss them

176
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:20,920
or you don't know how you're going to deal with this week,

177
00:11:20,920 --> 00:11:23,360
talk about the new experiences

178
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:25,120
that your scout will have at camp

179
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:27,520
and all the positive potential involved.

180
00:11:27,719 --> 00:11:30,280
Now, anxiety usually comes from something

181
00:11:30,280 --> 00:11:32,839
that we don't know about or understand.

182
00:11:33,079 --> 00:11:37,479
So make sure to ask the scouts who are going to camp

183
00:11:37,479 --> 00:11:40,439
with your scout all the questions you like-

184
00:11:40,719 --> 00:11:42,959
And there are no silly questions,

185
00:11:43,199 --> 00:11:44,800
Really there are none.

186
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:48,839
If you have a concern or an anxiety or a worry

187
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:50,960
about exactly what's going to happen at camp,

188
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:54,040
get one of the scouts who's going and sit down

189
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:56,360
and ask them all of those questions.

190
00:11:56,360 --> 00:11:58,000
Get them to explain things to you

191
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:59,360
and it's going to make you feel better.

192
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:02,080
It's always better to ask than to worry,

193
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:04,720
And I have talked with a lot of parents

194
00:12:04,720 --> 00:12:07,920
sending their scout down to camp for the first time

195
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:11,160
who are concerned that I'm going to think they're silly

196
00:12:11,160 --> 00:12:13,000
or they're overreacting

197
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
or that they're asking questions

198
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,680
that I haven't heard before.

199
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
It goes a long, long way

200
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:24,160
If you can sit down, be patient with a parent

201
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:27,400
and do your best to answer all of the questions

202
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:28,640
that they may have.

203
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:31,560
Now, as a parent, you need to understand

204
00:12:31,560 --> 00:12:34,400
and respect the way that your scout

205
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:37,800
will be communicating with you while they're at camp.

206
00:12:37,920 --> 00:12:39,920
Now, in the age of cell phones and things,

207
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:42,680
you know, it's going to depend a lot upon

208
00:12:42,760 --> 00:12:45,800
the way that the scouts who are taking your scout

209
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:49,640
to camp are going to work within that technology.

210
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:52,440
Will they be writing letters home?

211
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:54,480
Will the letters get home in time?

212
00:12:54,480 --> 00:12:56,800
Will you be able to check in with the camp

213
00:12:56,800 --> 00:12:58,400
or with the scouter?

214
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:01,240
Look, if you're really anxious about this

215
00:13:01,240 --> 00:13:04,240
and you're anxious about being out of touch

216
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:07,560
with your scout for a long period of time like this,

217
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:09,400
talk to the scouter about it

218
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:14,040
and see what you can do to set up a communication plan

219
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:17,199
so that you can check in with the scouter or the scout

220
00:13:17,199 --> 00:13:22,079
and realize that that's important both to your state of mind

221
00:13:22,079 --> 00:13:25,280
at home and to your scout's state of mind at camp.

222
00:13:25,520 --> 00:13:28,000
And then, finally, my suggestion to you as a parent:

223
00:13:28,319 --> 00:13:30,839
if you are sending your scout off to camp,

224
00:13:30,839 --> 00:13:33,360
plant something wonderful to do while they're away.

225
00:13:35,280 --> 00:13:38,040
You may have siblings at home that you need to look after.

226
00:13:38,040 --> 00:13:40,000
You have your regular daily routine

227
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:41,640
that you're going to follow and everything.

228
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:43,439
Take advantage of the little bit of freedom

229
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:46,360
that this is going to afford you and make some plans.

230
00:13:46,480 --> 00:13:47,280
It'll be great.

231
00:13:47,280 --> 00:13:49,720
So I understand that. look,

232
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:53,440
I know when parents are anxious about these types of things

233
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:55,160
and I don't laugh it off.

234
00:13:55,160 --> 00:13:57,040
I don't think you're being silly,

235
00:13:57,040 --> 00:13:59,000
I don't think you're being childish,

236
00:13:59,520 --> 00:14:02,760
because this is a big challenge, not only for your scout,

237
00:14:02,760 --> 00:14:05,600
but it can be a very big challenge for parents too.

238
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:08,280
Once again, think positively

239
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:10,200
And, as you're preparing your scout to go to camp,

240
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:12,440
talk about all the wonderful things that are going to happen

241
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:16,640
and the wonderful potential and possibilities of the week at camp.

242
00:14:16,840 --> 00:14:20,480
Try and stay away from telling them how much you're going to miss them

243
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:22,080
or how worried you're going to be.

244
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:25,280
